    "summary": "High‑energy China>Rider, soaring Other One and expansive Bird Song",
    "blurb": "The show bursts with a smoking China Cat Sunflower → I Know You Rider transition and a monster The Other One. The second set opens with an explosive Bird Song that leads into tight jams on Sugar Magnolia, Wharf Rat and Hard To Handle. Jerry’s soaring tone and Bill’s powerful drumming drive the high‑energy performance.",
    "review": "April 14, 1971 finds the Dead at Christy Mathewson Stadium firing on all cylinders. The first set builds momentum with classic road staples before launching into a blistering China Cat Sunflower → I Know You Rider segue that showcases Garcia’s soaring, wah‑wah‑infused leads and Weir’s riff‑laden solo. From there the energy never lets up – “The Other One” erupts into a monster jam powered by Bill Kreutzmann’s thunderous drum solo, while Phil Lesh’s bass anchors every twist.\n\nThe second set kicks off with an explosive Bird Song that stretches out beautifully, followed by a sweetly funky Sugar Magnolia and a seamless flow into Wharf Rat and Hard To Handle. The band stays locked in, delivering crisp improvisations despite the occasional tape static. Pigpen’s organ sits low in the mix but adds his trademark vocal harmonies when they surface. The night closes with an exuberant encore of Uncle John’s Band, leaving the crowd humming as the lights come up.",
